A||||6||Songman||Story of the dancing horse...||||7||
Z||000000||Songman||08-12-2001||10:54 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.dropbears.com/m/models/ferrari/images/flogo_onred.gif"> <P>"The story of the prancing horse is simple and fascinating. The horse was painted on the fuselage of the fighter plane flown by Francesco Baracca, a heroic Italian pilot who died on Mount Montello: the Italian ace of aces of the First World War. [Flying Spads and Nieuports he won 35 combats.] <BR>In 1923, when I won the first Savio circuit, which was run in Ravenna, I met Count Enrico Baracca, the pilot's father, and subsequently his mother, Countess Paolina. <P>One day she said to me, "Ferrari, why don't you put my son's prancing horse on your cars; it would bring you luck." I still have Baracca's photograph with the dedication by his parents, in which they entrusted the emblem to me. The horse was black and has remained so; I added the canary yellow background because it is the colour of Modena." <P>Enzo Ferrari<BR> <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||7
Z||000001||Raydar||08-12-2001||11:00 AM||||Cool!<BR>Thanks for sharing that piece of history.<P>They have to use entire marketing departments to come up with that sort of thing, today.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||08-12-2001||01:30 PM||||Steve, you've probably heard of Shaw Industries up in Dalton...<P>Well, what became Shaw Industries started out as Star Paper Tube and was started by Bob Shaw's father. When he took over the company in the late 60s, they moved into carpet production and then bought out Philadephia Carpets which was a fairly big venture back then.<P>Once at a management dinner about 12 years ago, Mr Shaw was relating this history of the company to us. He said that they wanted Shaw to be a world power in the carpet industry so they hired a high priced advertising firm out of New York to come up with the pitch. <P>About 6 months and several hundred thousand dollars later the NY agency was ready to show Mr Shaw and the Board their ideas. They put the Philadelphia Carpets Liberty Bell logo inside the Star Paper Tube star logo, and called the new company  Shaw Industries, Incorporated.<P>So much for entire marketing departments! hehe A child with crayons could have thought that one up, but it did work!<P>Not as exciting as Ferrari, but still a kind of funny story.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif">  <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A><p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000003||Formula88||08-12-2001||03:01 PM||||That reminds me of the story of how the Shelby GT350 name came about.  They called it Shelby, duh, because it's Carroll Shelby's baby.  And GT was pretty straight forward, but where'd the 350 come from?  It was 350' from the garage to the test track where they did their testing.  When Shelby suggested the name and they questioned him on it, he replied, "If it's a good car, the name won't matter.  If it's a bad car, the name won't save it."<P>And my favorite car (well, tire) ad of all time goes to Goodyear.  You see a Ferrari racing through the hills of Maranello Italy as the voice over says, "Every Ferrari that leaves the Ferrari factory in Maranello Italy does so on Goodyear Eagle tires.   Why?" ...the camera switches to a low stree-level angle as you see the car screech to a halt just in front of a pair of legs.  The camera pans up and you see Enzo Ferrari standing there - supremely proud as the narrator adds, "Because Mr. Ferrari wants it that way."<P>God, I love that ad!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000004||hugh||08-12-2001||05:49 PM||||Does anybody know how Pegasus came to be in the Fiero Logo?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||08-12-2001||11:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>Does anybody know how Pegasus came to be in the Fiero Logo?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The Fiero was originally going to be called Pegasus.  Hence, the logo.  I'm glad the kept it.  It's a very cool logo.  Besides, what would you use for Fiero?  I like the sound of Fiero better, too.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||08-12-2001||11:17 PM||||I think its kinda cool how we have our own little "animal" that goes along with our car.<P>Ferrari has the horse<BR>Lamborghini the bull<BR>Fiero the Pegasus<P>And the Pegasus is something that's never been seen before, kinda like how the Fiero was when it was first introduced, a completely different and exotic (sorta) car.||||reg||1
